# The script language as the shipped sources use it

Generated by `lib/gen_grammar_md.py` from `lib/grammar.md.in`. The prose and the
EBNF are written by hand; **every number is filled in from the output of a
script** in `lib/`, so the text cannot drift from the proofs. Regenerate with
`python -I lib/run_language.py` instead of editing.

Subject: the dialect of the {{ps sources}} `.java` sources of Street Legal Racing:
Redline, Build 940 (`D:\SLRR-test`, read-only), which the game's own compiler
turns into `.class` files of format TUFA. This document is the language half of
the compiler work: what the scanner and the parser have to accept, and how
often each construct occurs. The class format and the opcodes are in
`notes/foundation.md` and `notes/opcodes.md`.

Evidence standard, as in `foundation.md`: **proven** = a script checks the
statement against the whole corpus and the count is quoted; **observed** = seen
in examples; **unknown** = said outright. A construct with count 0 is called
*never used*: `parser.py` may still accept it, but nothing in the corpus says
what the game's compiler does with it.

## 1. Result

| Deliverable | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `lib/lexer.py` | Tokenizer: identifiers, keywords, int / float / string / resource id literals, operators, comments |
| `lib/parser.py` | Recursive descent parser with precedence climbing; plain AST, one `Node` class, every node with its token range |
| `lib/unparse.py` | AST back to tokens (used by the proof that the AST loses nothing) |
| `lib/symbols.py` | AST to the rows of CLSS / FILD / MTHD: names, flags, descriptors, tree numbers, locals word; type name resolution |
| `lib/shape.py` | The postfix shape of a method, once from the AST and once from the class file |
| `lib/check_parse.py` | Proof 1: every source tokenizes and parses |
| `lib/check_members.py` | Proof 2: classes, fields and methods found by the parser = class files |
| `lib/check_shape.py` | Proof 3: expression and statement structure found by the parser = class files |
| `lib/check_sensitivity.py` | Proof 4: a wrong grammar would have been noticed, and where it could not be |
| `lib/census.py` | How often every construct occurs, with examples (`notes/grammar_census.txt`, `.json`) |
| `lib/study_class_dirs.py` | Where the classes a source names live |
| `lib/run_language.py` | Runs all of it and regenerates this file |

Headline numbers:

- **{{p L1}} sources tokenize and {{p P1}} parse** ({{ps bytes}} bytes,
  {{c lines}} lines, {{ps tokens}} tokens, {{ps comments}} comments, {{ps nodes}} AST nodes).
  There is no failure to list. The tokens regenerated from the AST equal the
  tokens read for {{p P3}} sources.
- **The declarations agree with the class files for all {{ms pairs}} pairs**
  ({{ms classes}} classes, {{ms field rows}} field rows, {{ms method rows}} method rows):
  {{mx rules}} rules, every one without exception (section 10.2). No mismatch
  exists.
- **The structure agrees too**: of {{sh stats.trees}} trees (field initialisers
  and method bodies) {{sh stats.trees equal}} have exactly the postfix shape
  the AST predicts, jump targets included. The {{sh cat.pair_940 (stale).different}} +
  {{sh cat.pair_other (stale).different}} that differ in stale pairs are the edits already
  listed in `corpus.md`; the single one in a consistent pair is a resource id
  whose pack does not exist (section 10.3).
- **The dialect is a small subset of Java 1.0 plus one literal.** A Java parser
  would stop at {{c note.rid-literal}} resource id literals, {{c note.body-semicolon}}
  bodiless concrete methods, {{c note.import-colon}} imports with a colon and
  {{c note.native-field}} + {{c note.abstract-field}} fields declared
  `native` / `abstract` (section 8).
- **The {{cat source_only}} sources without a class use no construct that the
  pairs do not exercise**: of the {{c census keys with a source file}} construct
  keys of the census, {{os count}} occur only in such sources
  (`grammar_census.txt`, section "constructs that occur only in sources
  without a class file"), and every type name in them resolves.

## 2. Lexical grammar

`lexer.py` is strict: a character no rule accepts raises `LexError`. Proven by
`check_parse.py`: L1 {{p L1}} sources tokenize; L2 tokens, comments and white
space tile the file for {{p L2}}; L3 the token stream equals that of the
foundation's independent `srclex.py` for {{p L3}}.

### 2.1 Characters and lines

All sources are 7-bit ASCII with CRLF line ends (`corpus.md`). White space is
blank, TAB, CR and LF (the lexer also takes FF and VT). A line number is 1 +
the number of LF characters before the token; the compiler stamps every node
with one (`foundation.md` 3.6). Outside comments and strings only letters,
digits, `_` and the operator characters of 2.8 occur: the lexer has no rule
for `@ # $ \` or a backquote, and rule L1 holds.

### 2.2 Comments

| Form | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| `// ...` to the end of the line | {{c comment.line}} |
| `/* ... */` | {{c comment.block}} |
| of these over several lines | {{c comment.block over several lines}} |
| `/* ... */` holding `//` | {{c comment.block holding //}} |
| `// ...` holding `/*` (does not open a block comment) | {{c comment.line holding /*}} |
| `/* ... */` holding another `/*` (nesting) | {{z comment.block holding /*}} |
| string literal holding `//` or `/*` (not a comment) | {{c string holding // or /*}} |

A block comment ends at the first `*/`. Whether the scanner nests is not
observable. A comment may stand between any two tokens
(`public class Init extends GameType /*implements Runnable*/`). Every block
comment is closed (rule L1) and {{z comment.line ending in a backslash}} line
comments end in a backslash.

### 2.3 Identifiers and keywords

```
Identifier = Letter { Letter | Digit } ;        (* and not a keyword *)
Letter     = "A".."Z" | "a".."z" | "_" ;
Digit      = "0".."9" ;
```

{{c token.id}} identifier tokens, {{c token.kw}} keyword tokens.

{{table keywords}}

`protected` and `synchronized` are in the list because class files without
source carry the flags 0x2 and 0x80 (`foundation.md` 3.3); no shipped source
uses them. `int`, `float` and `void` are the only primitive types: every
descriptor of every blob of the corpus, runtime library included, is built
from `I`, `F`, `V`, `L<class>;` and at most one `[` (rule D1 of
`check_members.py`, {{m D1}} blobs; {{mx I}} `I`, {{mx F}} `F`, {{mx V}} `V`,
{{mx L}} class descriptors). There is no `boolean`, `char`, `byte`, `short`,
`long`, `double`, `try`, `catch`, `throw`, `goto`. No source uses
one of the other Java 1.0 reserved words as an identifier
({{z note.java-reserved-id}} uses), so whether the scanner reserves them is not
observable. `String` is a class (`java.lang.String`), not a keyword.

### 2.4 Integer literals

```
IntLiteral = Digit { Digit }
           | "0" ( "x" | "X" ) HexDigit { HexDigit } ;
HexDigit   = Digit | "A".."F" | "a".."f" ;
```

| Form | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| decimal | {{c form.int decimal}} |
| hexadecimal `0x` | {{c form.int hex 0x}} |
| hexadecimal `0X` | {{c form.int hex 0X}} |
| hexadecimal above 0x7FFFFFFF (`0xFFFFFFFF`, `0x80000000`) | {{c int hex above 0x7FFFFFFF}} |
| decimal with a leading zero (octal in Java) | {{z int.decimal with a leading zero}} |
| decimal above 2147483647 | {{z odd.decimal int literal above 2147483647}} |
| any literal above 32 bits | {{z odd.int literal above 32 bits}} |

Hex literals have 2, 3, 4, 6 or 8 digits. No `L` suffix exists (it would not
tokenize). The value is the number modulo 2**32 and becomes the immediate of an
`int` carrier node: **proven** for {{sh lit.int literals}} of {{sh lit.int literals}}
int literals in the trees of consistent pairs (`check_shape.py` compares the
value), {{sh lit.int literals written in hex}} of them hexadecimal,
{{sh lit.int literals above 0x7FFFFFFF (hex)}} above 0x7FFFFFFF. A minus sign is
not part of the literal: `-1` is the literal 1 under a unary minus
({{c unary.- applied to int literal}} + {{c unary.- applied to float literal}} signed
literals, and {{c unary.+ applied to float literal}} with a unary plus).

### 2.5 Floating literals

```
FloatLiteral = Digit { Digit } "." Digit { Digit } [ "f" ]
             | Digit { Digit } "f" ;
```

| Form | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| `12.5` | {{c form.float d.d}} |
| `12.5f` | {{c form.float d.df}} |
| `10000000f` | {{c form.float df}} |
| with an exponent (`1e5`) | {{z float.with an exponent}} |
| without digits before the point (`.5`) | {{z float.without digits before the point}} |
| without digits after the point (`5.`) | {{z float.without digits after the point}} |
| suffix `F`, `d` or `D` | {{z float.with a suffix other than f}} |

Examples of the suffix forms:

{{ex form.float df}}

A literal with a point is a `float` whether or not it has the suffix: the
dialect has no `double`. {{c float with a leading zero}} literal has a leading zero
(`08.500`) and {{c float with more than 9 integer digits}} have more than 9
integer digits (`1000000000000000.0`; the compiler's conversion wraps the
integer part modulo 2**32, `foundation.md` section 6). The bit pattern the
compiler produces is **not** the correctly rounded value in about 19% of the
cases; that is question 1 of `foundation.md` and is not settled here.
`check_shape.py` compares float literals by kind and position only
({{sh lit.float literals}} of them).

### 2.6 String literals; no character literals

```
StringLiteral = '"' { any character except '"', '\' and line end | '\' any character } '"' ;
```

| Property | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| string literals | {{c string}} |
| empty `""` | {{c string empty}} |
| escape `\"` | {{c string escape \"}} |
| escape `\\` | {{c string escape \\}} |
| escape `\n` | {{c string escape \n}} |
| escape `\t` | {{c string escape \t}} |
| any other escape | {{z string.with an escape other than quote, backslash, n, t}} |
| literals holding a raw TAB character | {{c string with a raw TAB}} |
| literals of 100 characters and more | {{c string length 100-999}} + {{c string length 1000+}} |
| characters outside printable ASCII and TAB | {{z string.with a character outside printable ASCII and TAB}} |
| character literals `'x'` | {{z char literal}} |

A string does not span lines. The pool text is the literal after escape
processing (`\n` is one LF, `\t` one TAB, `\"` and `\\` the character itself):
**proven** for {{sh lit.str literals}} of {{sh lit.str literals}} string literals of the
consistent pairs, which hold every escape of the corpus
({{sh lit.escapes \"}}, {{sh lit.escapes \\}}, {{sh lit.escapes \n}}, {{sh lit.escapes \t}})
and all {{sh lit.str literals holding a raw TAB}} raw-TAB literals. What the scanner
does with another escaped character is unknown; `lexer.string_value` lets it
stand for itself.

### 2.7 The resource id literal

```
ResourceId = Identifier { "." Identifier } ":" "0" ( "x" | "X" ) HexDigit { HexDigit } "r" ;
```

One token, no white space inside: `cars.racers.Axis:0x0000FFB4r`,
`frontend:0x007Dr`, `parts:0x000053FFr`. {{c note.rid-literal}} uses in
{{f note.rid-literal}} sources. Since the lexer has no other rule for a hex
number followed by `r`, rule L1 proves that no literal is written with a blank
around the colon and that `0x...r` never stands without a pack name.

| Property | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| 8 hex digits | {{c form.rid 0x, 8 hex digits}} |
| 4 hex digits | {{c form.rid 0x, 4 hex digits}} |
| 7 hex digits | {{c form.rid 0x, 7 hex digits}} |
| 9 hex digits (value still below 2**32) | {{c form.rid 0x, 9 hex digits}} |
| with lower-case hex digits | {{c rid with lower-case hex digits}} |
| pack name of 1 / 2 / 3 / 4 / 5 identifiers | {{c rid pack segments 1}} / {{c rid pack segments 2}} / {{c rid pack segments 3}} / {{c rid pack segments 4}} / {{c rid pack segments 5}} |
| distinct pack spellings | {{c rid packs written (distinct spellings)}} |
| distinct `.rpk` files they name | {{c rid packs on disk (distinct files named)}} |
| literals whose pack is spelled in another letter case than the file | {{c rid literals spelled in another letter case than the file}} |
| literals whose pack has no file | {{c rid literals of packs without a file on disk}} |

**The pack name is the path of an `.rpk` file** relative to the game
directory, with `.` for the path separator and without the extension:
`cars.racers.Axis` is `cars\racers\Axis.rpk`, `multibot.maps.A1Ring.t_data` is
`multibot\maps\A1Ring\t_data.rpk`. Every part of the name is an identifier in
the corpus (letters, digits, `_`; `stdpack_231`, `ROC_Track`). The local id is
the hex number. The literal compiles to an `rid` carrier whose pool entry holds
the pack file name and the id: the id is **proven** for
{{sh lit.rid literals}} rid literals of the consistent pairs (all but the one of
section 10.3), among them {{sh lit.rid literals with 9 hex digits}} with 9 digits
and {{sh lit.rid literals with 7 hex digits}} with 7. The letter case of the pack
name in the pool is not always the source's (`foundation.md` question 3); the
spellings that differ from the file on disk:

{{table packs}}

A resource id is an `int` value (observed, `docs/ENGINE.md` 4.1): it is
assigned to `int` fields and array elements and passed to `int` parameters,
and it is also what `new ResourceRef(...)` takes.

### 2.8 Operators and punctuation

Longest match. {{c token.op}} tokens.

{{table operators}}

`>>>`, `>>>=`, `<<=`, `>>=` and `%=` are never used; the lexer knows them
because the compiler's expression kinds have slots for them
(`opcodes.md` table 2: 0x0C, 0x26, 0x29-0x2B).

## 3. Syntactic grammar

EBNF: `=` defines, `|` separates alternatives, `[ ]` is optional, `{ }` is
repetition, quoted text is a token. This is the grammar the corpus needs; it
is what `parser.py` implements, minus the alternatives listed in 3.4 that no
source uses.

### 3.1 Declarations

```
Unit            = "package" QualifiedName ";" { Import } { TypeDecl } ;
Import          = "import" [ Identifier ":" ] QualifiedName "." "*" ";" ;
QualifiedName   = Identifier { "." Identifier } ;

TypeDecl        = { Modifier } "class" Identifier [ "extends" Identifier ]
                  [ "implements" Identifier { "," Identifier } ] "{" { Member } "}"
                | { Modifier } "interface" Identifier "{" "}" ;
Modifier        = "public" | "private" | "static" | "final" | "abstract" | "native" ;
Member          = FieldDecl | MethodDecl | ConstructorDecl ;

FieldDecl       = { Modifier } Type Declarator { "," Declarator } ";" ;
Declarator      = Identifier [ "=" Expression ] ;
MethodDecl      = { Modifier } Type Identifier "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ( Block | ";" ) ;
ConstructorDecl = { Modifier } Identifier "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ( Block | ";" ) ;
Param           = Type Identifier ;
Type            = ( "int" | "float" | "void" | Identifier ) [ "[" "]" ] ;
```

A member that starts (after its modifiers) with `Identifier "("` is a
constructor; every other member starts with a type.

### 3.2 Statements

```
Block           = "{" { Statement } "}" ;
Statement       = Block
                | LocalDecl ";"
                | StatementExpr ";"
                | "if" "(" Expression ")" Statement [ "else" Statement ]
                | "while" "(" Expression ")" Statement
                | "do" Statement "while" "(" Expression ")" ";"
                | "for" "(" [ LocalDecl | StatementExpr ] ";" [ Expression ] ";" [ StatementExpr ] ")" Statement
                | "switch" "(" Expression ")" "{" { SwitchGroup } "}"
                | "break" ";"
                | "continue" ";"
                | "return" [ Expression ] ";" ;
LocalDecl       = Type Declarator { "," Declarator } ;
SwitchGroup     = ( "case" Expression ":" | "default" ":" ) { Statement } ;
StatementExpr   = Assignment
                | ( "++" | "--" ) Unary
                | Postfix ( "++" | "--" )
                | MethodCall
                | ( "this" | "super" ) Arguments ;       (* first statement of a constructor *)
```

A statement that starts with `int` or `float`, or with
`Identifier [ "[" "]" ] Identifier`, is a declaration; everything else that
does not start with a statement keyword is an expression statement.

### 3.3 Expressions

```
Expression      = Conditional | Assignment ;
Assignment      = Target AssignOp Expression ;                      (* groups to the right *)
Target          = a Postfix that is a name, a field access or an array element ;
AssignOp        = "=" | "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "&=" | "|=" | "^=" ;
Conditional     = Binary [ "?" Expression ":" Conditional ] ;
Binary          = Unary { BinaryOp Unary | "instanceof" Identifier } ;   (* by the levels of 6.1 *)
Unary           = ( "+" | "-" | "!" | "~" | "++" | "--" ) Unary
                | "(" ( "int" | "float" ) ")" Unary                 (* cast to a primitive type *)
                | "(" Identifier ")" UnaryNotPlusMinus              (* cast to a class, see 6.3 *)
                | Postfix [ "++" | "--" ] ;
Postfix         = Primary { "." Identifier [ Arguments ] | "[" Expression "]" } ;
Primary         = Literal
                | Identifier [ Arguments ]
                | "this"
                | "super" "." Identifier Arguments
                | "(" Expression ")"
                | "new" Identifier Arguments
                | "new" ( "int" | "float" | Identifier ) "[" Expression "]" ;
MethodCall      = a Postfix that ends in Arguments ;
Arguments       = "(" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] ")" ;
Literal         = IntLiteral | FloatLiteral | StringLiteral | ResourceId
                | "null" | "true" | "false" ;
```

`UnaryNotPlusMinus` is a `Unary` that does not start with `+`, `-`, `++` or
`--`. Nothing may follow an array creation `new T[n]` directly (no `.`, no
`[`).

### 3.4 What `parser.py` accepts beyond this, and no source uses

Each of these is parsed (most with a note in `unit.notes`), has **0** uses, and
is therefore untested against the game's compiler:

| Construct | Uses |
| --- | ---: |
| `import a.b.C;` (no `.*`) | {{z note.import-single}} |
| an import after a type declaration; a `;` between top-level declarations | {{z note.import-after-type}}; {{z note.stray-semicolon-top}} |
| source without `package`, or with something before it | {{z note.no-package}}; {{z unit.package not first}} |
| `interface` with members or with `extends` | {{z class.interface with members}}; {{z class.interface with extends or implements}} |
| `extends` with a dotted name or with a list | {{z class.extends a dotted name}}; {{z note.extends-list}} |
| a `;` between class members | {{z note.stray-semicolon-member}} |
| `static { ... }` or `{ ... }` initialiser block in a class | {{z node.Initializer}} |
| a method without return type whose name is not the class name | {{z note.ctor-name}} |
| the same modifier twice | {{z note.modifier-repeated}} |
| `abstract` method; a body after `native` | {{z method.abstract}}; {{z note.native-body}} |
| brackets after a name (`int a[]`), after a parameter list, more than one `[]` | {{z array.brackets after the field name}} + {{z array.brackets after the local name}} + {{z array.brackets after the parameter name}}; {{z note.method-dims}}; {{z note.multi-dim}} |
| a class type written with dots (`java.util.Vector v`) | {{z type.written with dots}} |
| `final` (or any modifier) on a local variable or a parameter | {{z local.with a modifier}}; {{z param.final}} |
| array initialiser `{ a, b }` | {{z note.array-init}} |
| empty statement `;` | {{z stmt.empty statement}} |
| labelled statement, `break label`, `continue label` | {{z note.label}} |
| a declaration as the unbraced body of `if` / `else` / a loop | {{z note.decl-as-substatement}} |
| several expressions in a `for` header (`i = 0, j = 1`) | {{z for.init with several expressions}}; {{z for.update with several expressions}} |
| several labels before one group (`case 1: case 2:`); a group that falls through | {{z switch.group with several labels}}; {{z switch.group falls through into the next}} |
| `default` not in the last group; a `switch` without groups | {{z switch.default not in the last group}}; {{z switch.empty body}} |
| an expression statement that is not an assignment, a call or `++` / `--` | {{z note.expr-statement}} |
| `new T(...);` as a statement | {{z exprstmt.new object}} |
| `this(...)` / `super(...)` elsewhere than as first statement of a constructor | {{z odd.this(...) or super(...) not as first statement}} + {{z odd.this(...) or super(...) outside a constructor}} + {{z odd.this(...) or super(...) inside an expression}} |
| assignment or `++` / `--` applied to something that is not a name, field or element | {{z note.assign-target}}; {{z note.incdec-target}} |
| a compound assignment whose value is used | {{z assign.compound operator used as a value}} |
| `?:` inside `?:` | {{z cond-expr.nested}} |
| cast to an array type; `instanceof` with an array or primitive type | {{z cast.to an array type}}; {{z instanceof.array or primitive type}} |
| `new T[a][b]`, `new T[n][]`, `new T[] { ... }` | {{z newarray.not exactly one size}} |
| `super.field` | {{z super.field access}} |
| operators `>>>` `>>>=` `<<=` `>>=` `%=` | {{z operator >>>}} {{z operator >>>=}} {{z operator <<=}} {{z operator >>=}} {{z operator %=}} |

Two of these do occur in class files without source: one `new T(...);`
statement (`Frontend`) and methods flagged abstract (5 rows).

## 4. Declarations in detail

### 4.1 Compilation unit, package

Every source starts with `package`; {{c unit.package}} of {{c unit}}. The class
name the compiler writes is package + `.` + declared name (rule C1,
{{m C1}}). The package does not follow from the directory: {{dx packages}}
packages, {{dx off_classpath}} of them outside the directory the classpath
gives them (section 7.4), and the class `java.game.track_data` is declared in
{{dd java.game.track_data}} map directories, `java.game.cars.kit_Custom_body`
in {{dd java.game.cars.kit_Custom_body}} car directories.

{{c unit.types 1}} sources declare one type; {{c unit.several top-level types}}
declare several (up to 9), and the compiler writes one blob per type into the
one class file, in source order (rule U2, {{m U2}} pairs). The first type is
always named like the file ({{z unit.first type not named like the file}}
exceptions); the others are not, and {{c unit.public type not named like the file}}
of them are `public` (section 8).

### 4.2 Imports

{{c import}} imports, all of the form `import package.*;`. They are listed in
7.1. `import parts:part.root.BodyPart.*;` occurs {{c import.colon}} times:

{{ex import.colon}}

Both sources have a class file, so the compiler accepts the line; what it
makes of it is unknown (no class sits in a package of that name, and the name
leaves no trace in the class file). `parser.py` records the word before the
colon as `Import.pack`.

### 4.3 Classes and the one interface

{{c class.class}} classes and {{c class.interface}} interface
(`GameState`, empty body). {{c class.extends}} classes have `extends`
with one simple name; the {{c class.no extends}} without get
`java.lang.Object` as superclass, the interface gets none (rule C3,
{{m C3}}). {{c class.implements}} classes implement {{c class.implements 1 interfaces}}
× 1 or {{c class.implements 2 interfaces}} × 2 interfaces (rule C4).

{{table class_modifiers}}

The class file has no place for class modifiers (CLSS holds only the interface
flag), so they leave no trace. `abstract` classes:

{{ex class.modifiers [abstract public]}}

### 4.4 Fields

{{c field.declaration}} declarations with {{c field}} declarators (up to 14 in
one declaration; `int a, b = 1, c;`), {{c field.static}} static and
{{c field.instance}} instance fields. One row per declarator, statics and
instance fields in two vectors, each in source order (rules F1, F2).
{{c field.with initialiser}} declarators have an initialiser, which becomes a
tree of its own (F5, F6); a field without one has no tree. The initialiser is
any expression: literals mostly, {{c field.initialiser is new}} `new`,
{{c field.initialiser is newarray}} array creations, and
{{c field.initialiser is unary ++}} times `++cmd` in one source:

{{ex field.initialiser is unary ++}}

Types: `int` {{c field.type int}}, `float` {{c field.type float}}, class
{{c field.type class}}, `int[]` {{c field.type int[]}}, `float[]`
{{c field.type float[]}}, class`[]` {{c field.type class[]}}. Arrays are written
`T[] name` only.

{{table field_modifiers}}

The flags word of the row is the plain mask of the modifier keywords, in any
order (rule F3, {{m F3}}): `public` 0x1, `private` 0x4, `static` 0x8,
`abstract` 0x10, `final` 0x20, `native` 0x40. That holds for the `native` and
`abstract` fields too, which a Java compiler would refuse (section 8.4).

### 4.5 Methods, constructors, native methods

{{c method}} methods and {{c constructor}} constructors. A constructor row is
named `<init>` and its descriptor has no return part; rows are in source order
in the static and the instance vector (rules M1, M2, M5).

{{table method_modifiers}}

Constructors are `public` ({{c constructor.modifiers [public]}}) but for
{{c constructor.modifiers []}} without modifier. {{c class.without constructor}}
classes and the interface declare no constructor, and **the compiler adds
none** (rule M9, {{m M9}} classes of the pairs, {{ms classes with no constructor}}
of them without constructor). Classes with several constructors:
{{c class.constructors 2}} with two, {{c class.constructors 3+}} with three or
more.

Parameters are `Type name`; `int` {{c param.type int}}, `float`
{{c param.type float}}, class {{c param.type class}}, arrays
{{c array.brackets after the parameter type}}. Return types: `void`
{{c method.return type void}}, `int` {{c method.return type int}}, `float`
{{c method.return type float}}, class {{c method.return type class}}, arrays
{{c method.return type int[]}} + {{c method.return type class[]}}.

**Native methods**: {{c method.native}} declarations in {{f method.native}}
sources, `native` anywhere among the modifiers, the body replaced by `;`
({{c method.native instance}} instance, {{c method.native static}} static). The
row carries 0x40 and an empty tree (rules M3, M7).

**Bodies.** What the three body forms compile to (rule M7, {{m M7}} rows):

{{table body_forms}}

So `;` is legal after any method or constructor head and gives an empty tree;
`{}` gives an empty tree for a method but the implicit `super()` call and a
`ret` for a constructor. This settles question 7 of `foundation.md`. In all
sources: methods `;` {{c method.body ";"}}, `{}` {{c method.body "{}"}};
constructors `;` {{c constructor.body ";"}}, `{}` {{c constructor.body "{}"}}.

**Constructor chaining.** {{c constructor.begins with super(...)}} constructors
begin with `super(...)`, {{c constructor.begins with this(...)}} with
`this(...)`, {{c constructor.without explicit this/super call (implicit super())}}
with neither. In the last case the compiler emits `push_int 0; super_init;
stmt ctor` itself before the first statement (proven by `check_shape.py`: the
trees agree only with that rule).

**Trees and the locals word.** The trees of a blob are numbered in source
order over fields with initialiser and methods (rules F6, M6, T1). The fifth
word of a method row is `this` + parameters + the deepest nesting of declared
locals, 0 without code (rule M8, {{m M8}}). The scopes that make it come out
right are in 5.2.

## 5. Statements in detail

{{table nodes}}

(All node kinds of the AST, declarations and expressions included; counted
over all sources.)

### 5.1 Forms

- **Expression statements** {{c node.ExprStmt}}: assignment `=`
  {{c exprstmt.assignment =}}, compound assignment
  {{c exprstmt.assignment +=}} + {{c exprstmt.assignment -=}} + {{c exprstmt.assignment *=}} +
  {{c exprstmt.assignment /=}} + {{c exprstmt.assignment |=}} + {{c exprstmt.assignment &=}} +
  {{c exprstmt.assignment ^=}}, call {{c exprstmt.call}}, `x++` {{c exprstmt.postfix ++}},
  `x--` {{c exprstmt.postfix --}}, `++x` {{c exprstmt.prefix ++}}, `--x`
  {{c exprstmt.prefix --}}, `super(...)` {{c exprstmt.super(...)}}, `this(...)`
  {{c exprstmt.this(...)}}. Nothing else.
- **Local declarations** {{c local.declaration}} statements with
  {{c local}} variables in all (the {{c local.in for header}} of `for` headers
  included); up to 11 declarators in one statement; {{c local.with initialiser}}
  with initialiser, {{c local.without initialiser}} without.
- **`if`** {{c node.If}}: {{c if.without else}} without `else`, {{c if.with else}}
  with, {{c if.else if chain}} of those as `else if`. The body is a block
  {{c substmt.if body is a block}} times and a single statement
  {{c substmt.if body is a single statement}} times. An `else` belongs to the
  nearest `if`: {{c if.dangling else (bound to the inner if)}} places need the
  rule (section 6.5).
- **`for`** {{c node.For}}: init is a declaration `int i = ...`
  {{c for.init declaration with 1 declarator}} times (always one `int`), one
  assignment {{c for.init 1 expression}} times, empty {{c for.init empty}} times;
  {{c for.without condition}} have no condition (`for(;;)`); the update is one
  expression {{c for.update 1 expression}} times (`i++`
  {{c for.update expression is Postfix ++}}, `i--`
  {{c for.update expression is Postfix --}}, `+=`
  {{c for.update expression is Assign +=}}) and empty {{c for.update empty}} times.
- **`while`** {{c node.While}}, **`do ... while`** {{c node.Do}}.
- **`switch`** {{c node.Switch}} with {{c switch.group}} groups:
  {{c switch.case label}} `case` labels and {{c switch.default label}} `default`
  labels, always one label per group, `default` always last. Every group ends
  with `break` ({{c switch.group ends with break}}) except the last group of
  {{c switch.last group ends without break}} switches, so nothing falls
  through. The case value is written in parentheses, `case(CMD_OK):`,
  {{c switch.case value written in parentheses}} times and plain
  {{c switch.case value written plain}} times; it is an int literal
  {{c switch.case value is int literal}} times, a name
  {{c switch.case value is a name}} times, a dotted name
  {{c switch.case value is a name (dotted)}} times and a sum
  {{c switch.case value is Binary}} times (`case(CMD_OPTION+1):`). The compiler
  evaluates case values at run time (a `case` node per label), so they need
  not be constants; the subject of one switch is a `String` field compared
  with `String` fields (`ExhaustTip.java`, `opcodes.md` section 7).
- **`break`** {{c node.Break}}, **`continue`** {{c node.Continue}} (in a `for`
  loop: {{c continue.in a for loop}}), **`return`** {{c node.Return}}
  ({{c return.with value}} with a value, {{c return.without value}} without).
- A **block** as a statement of its own: {{c stmt.Block as a statement of its own}};
  empty blocks `{}`: {{c stmt.Block empty}}.

### 5.2 Scopes

The locals word (rule M8) fixes where a declared local stops to occupy a slot.
It is right for all {{ms method rows}} method rows under this scoping, and
wrong for the stated number of methods under each alternative
(`check_members.py`):

| Scoping | Methods with a wrong locals word |
| --- | ---: |
| a local lives to the end of its block; a `for` variable to the end of the `for`; each `switch` group is a scope of its own | 0 |
| the same, but the whole `switch` body is one scope | {{sm switch is one scope}} |
| the same, but a `for` variable belongs to the enclosing block | {{sm for variable outlives the loop}} |
| no scopes: a local lives to the end of the method | {{sm no scopes}} |

So a local declared after `case A:` is released before `case B:` begins
(observed in the code: `release` in front of the `break` jump and again at
the next label); {{c switch.group that declares a local}} groups declare a
local. In Java the whole `switch` body is one scope;
{{c odd.local declared in two groups of one switch}} declarations repeat, in a
later group, a name an earlier group of the same switch declared. Java would
also reject the {{c odd.local hides an outer local or parameter}} declarations
that reuse the name of a live outer local or parameter (section 8.6); the
compiler gives each its own slot.

### 5.3 Conditions

A condition is any expression; the VM tests int and float against zero and
objects against null (`opcodes.md`, `jump_false`).

{{table conditions}}

Only "comparison", "logical", "negation" and "instanceof" would be `boolean`
in Java (and many of those negate or combine ints and objects). `while(1)`,
`if (1)`, `while( slots-- )`, `if (tab = the_car.getSfxTable(0))` are normal
here.

## 6. Expressions in detail

### 6.1 Binary operators: precedence and associativity

Ten levels, the order of Java. All group to the left. Level 1 binds weakest.

{{table precedence}}

Evidence. `check_shape.py` finds the postfix order of the operators and
operands in the class file equal to the order the AST predicts for every tree
of the consistent pairs. Because the code is postfix, two different groupings
of the same tokens give different sequences, so every place where two
operators share an operand without parentheses is a test. Such places, by
level of the operator applied last (rows) and first (columns):

{{table adjacency}}

Read: `a + b * c` and `a * b + c` together occur {{adj 9 10}} times and the
compiler multiplied first each time. A `.` means the two levels never share an
operand without parentheses; their order relative to each other is then
**not observable**. That is so for `|` against every other level (it stands
alone or between parenthesised operands), for `^` and `&` against most (the
sources write `(color & 0xFF) << 16`), for `==` against `<`, and for the
shifts against `<` and `+`. The Java order is the safe choice for
those, and it is the order of the compiler's own expression kind numbers
(`opcodes.md` table 2: level by level in the order of the Java grammar, from
`?:` 0x00 and `||` 0x01 to `* / %` 0x11-0x13, with an unused slot 0x0C where
`>>>` belongs).

Associativity: a chain `a op b op c` of one level occurs for the levels with
a count in the third column of the first table; grouping it to the right
changes the number of trees in the fourth column. For levels 4 to 8 no chain
exists, so their associativity is not observable.

What `check_sensitivity.py` measures when the whole table is wrong: all
operators on one level {{se flat.trees}} trees differ; everything grouped to
the right {{se right.trees}} trees.

{{table swaps}}

### 6.2 Unary operators, `++` and `--`

| Operator | Uses |
| --- | ---: |
| `-` | {{c unary.-}} ({{c unary.- applied to float literal}} on a float literal, {{c unary.- applied to int literal}} on an int literal, {{c unary.- applied to an expression}} on another expression) |
| `+` | {{c unary.+}} (emits no node) |
| `!` | {{c unary.!}} |
| `~` | {{c unary.~}} |
| `++x` | {{c unary.++}} ({{c incdec.++x as value}} as a value, {{c incdec.++x as statement}} as a statement) |
| `--x` | {{c unary.--}} ({{c incdec.--x as value}} / {{c incdec.--x as statement}}) |
| `x++` | {{c incdec.x++ as value}} as a value, {{c incdec.x++ as statement}} as a statement |
| `x--` | {{c incdec.x-- as value}} / {{c incdec.x-- as statement}} |

A prefix operator binds tighter than any binary operator: it is the left
operand of one {{sc prefix operator as left operand of a binary operator}} times, and
reading `-a * b` as `-(a * b)` changes {{se loose unary.trees}} trees, reading
`!a && b` as `!(a && b)` {{se loosest unary.trees}}. `!--x` occurs
{{c unary.! applied to unary --}} times. The operand of `++` / `--` is a name, a
field or an array element.

### 6.3 Casts

```
"(" ( "int" | "float" ) ")" Unary
"(" Identifier ")" UnaryNotPlusMinus
```

{{c node.Cast}} casts: to a class {{c cast.to class}}, to `int` {{c cast.to int}},
to `float` {{c cast.to float}}. The parser decides at the `(`: a parenthesised
primitive type is always a cast; a parenthesised single identifier is a cast
when the next token starts an operand: an identifier or literal
({{c cast.followed by id}} casts are followed by an identifier), `(`
({{c cast.followed by '('}}), `this`, `super`, `new`, `null`
({{c cast.followed by 'null'}}), `true`, `false`, `!`, `~`. Otherwise it is a
parenthesised expression: `(a) + b` and `case(CMD_OK):` are not casts
({{c paren.around Name}} parenthesised single names).

Evidence: without the identifier rule {{se no name cast.no_parse}} sources do
not parse at all ({{se no name cast.trees}} trees). A cast takes the whole
operand with its `.name`, `[i]` and `(args)` suffixes: `(Slider)m.gadget`
casts `m.gadget` ({{sc cast whose operand has a "." "[" or "(" suffix}} such
casts; reading it as `((Slider)m).gadget` changes
{{se cast of primary.trees}} trees), and it binds tighter than a binary
operator ({{sc cast as left operand of a binary operator}} places,
`(float)Config.video_x/(float)Config.video_y`). **Not observable**: whether
`(Name) -x` is a cast. No source has `+` or `-` after a parenthesised name
that could be a type ({{se cast before sign.trees}} trees change under the
other reading); `parser.py` follows Java and reads a subtraction.

A cast compiles to `expr cast` with a `class` carrier for a class and a `type`
carrier (`I`, `F`) for a primitive (`opcodes.md`).

### 6.4 `instanceof`, array creation, `new`, the conditional operator

- **`instanceof`** {{c node.InstanceOf}} uses, always with a simple class name,
  on the level of the relational operators.
- **Array creation** {{c node.NewArray}}: `new T[n]`, one dimension, never an
  initialiser list. Element type `int` {{c newarray.of int}}, `float`
  {{c newarray.of float}}, class {{c newarray.of class}}. The size is an int
  literal {{c newarray.size is int literal}} times, a name
  {{c newarray.size is name or field}}, a call {{c newarray.size is call}}, an
  arithmetic expression {{c newarray.size is arithmetic or bitwise operator}}.
  Array elements are read and written by `a[i]` ({{c node.Index}} uses);
  `a[i][j]` occurs {{z index.of an array element}} times, the VM has no
  multi-dimensional arrays.
- **Object creation** {{c node.New}}: `new Name(args)`, simple class name. It
  may be the receiver of a call (`new SfxRef(id).play();`,
  {{c call.on new object}} times).
- **`?:`** {{c node.Cond}} uses in {{f cond-expr}} sources:

{{ex cond-expr}}

  It binds weaker than every binary operator ({{sc conditional whose condition is a binary expression without parentheses}}
  place shows it: `nrTime2 < nrTime1 ? 0 : 1`; the other reading changes
  {{se cond tight.trees}} tree). Both branches are evaluated (`opcodes.md`).

### 6.5 Assignment

{{table assign_ops}}

Assignment groups to the right and its value can be used:
`a[0] = a[1] = x` ({{sc assignment whose value is an assignment (a = b = c)}}
places in the pairs; the left-grouping reading changes
{{se left assign.trees}} trees), as an argument
({{c assign.as a value in argument}}), as a condition
({{c assign.as a value in condition}}), as an operand
({{c assign.as a value in operand}}). Only `=` is ever used as a value. The
target is a name {{c assign.target Name}} times, an array element
{{c assign.target Index}} times, a field access {{c assign.target Field}} times.

The `else` rule of 5.1 is proven the same way: binding the `else` of
`if (a) if (b) x; else y;` to the outer `if` changes
{{se else outer.trees}} trees.

### 6.6 Names, field access, calls

A **name chain** is identifiers joined by dots, `a`, `a.b`, `a.b.c`:
{{c chain.name chains}} in all, {{c chain.of 1 identifier}} single names,
{{c chain.of 2 identifiers}} of two, {{c chain.of 3 identifiers}} of three,
{{c chain.of 4 identifiers}} of four, {{c chain.of 5 identifiers}} of five. The
parser cannot know whether the first identifier is a local, a field or a
class; the AST keeps the chain as nested `Field` nodes over a `Name`. In the
class file a chain of n identifiers is one node (n = 1) or one `path` of
n segments: **proven** for {{sh head.name paths}} name paths of the consistent
pairs ({{sh head.name paths whose node count differs from the number of identifiers}}
exceptions); {{sh head.name paths starting with a local node}} start with a
local, {{sh head.name paths starting with a field node}} with a field,
{{sh head.name paths starting with a class node}} with a class (7.3).

Field access on anything else is `getfield`:

{{table field_targets}}

Calls ({{c node.Call}}):

{{table call_targets}}

"none" is `f(args)`, "name" `a.f(args)`, "name chain" `a.b.f(args)`. Those
three compile to `call` + path; `super.f(args)` to `call_super`; every other
receiver is evaluated and the call is `call_on` (proven by `check_shape.py`,
events `call`, `call_super`, `call_on`). `this` occurs {{c node.This}} times:
as an argument {{c this.argument}}, as a receiver {{c this.receiver}}
(`this.x` {{c fieldaccess.on this}}, `this.f()` {{c call.on this}}), as an
assigned value {{c this.assigned value}}.

## 7. Names and classes

### 7.1 Imports that occur

{{table imports}}

All {{c import.star}} imports are on-demand imports of a package.
{{c import.of a package without classes}} name a package in which the corpus
has no class ({{c import.of the members of a class}} of them a class,
`java.lang.Integer.*`), {{c import.repeated}} repeat an import of the same
source, {{c import.own package}} import the source's own package,
{{c import.java.lang explicit}} import `java.lang`. Sources with such imports
have class files, so the compiler tolerates all of it.

### 7.2 How a type name becomes a class name

Type names are always single identifiers. The class file holds full names
(`Ljava.util.resource.ResourceRef;`), so the compiler resolves them.
`symbols.Resolver` does it like this:

1. a class declared in the same source, or another class of the source's
   package;
2. the packages of the imports, in source order;
3. `java.lang`.

Steps 1 and 3 are the **implicit packages**: the own package and `java.lang`
need no import.

Proof: the resolved name equals the name in the class file for every type
name the class file shows: superclass and interfaces (rules C3, C4), field
types (F4, {{m F4}}), parameter and return types (M5, {{m M5}}), and, through
`check_shape.py`, the types of locals, `new`, array creation, casts and
`instanceof` in every tree that agrees ({{sh ev.declare}} + {{sh ev.new}} +
{{sh ev.newarray}} + {{sh ev.cast}} + {{sh ev.instanceof}} type carriers). In the
sources of the pairs that is {{sh type.class type names}} class type names:
{{sh type.first found in file}} found in the same file,
{{sh type.first found in package}} in the package,
{{sh type.first found in import}} through an import,
{{sh type.first found in java.lang}} in `java.lang`,
{{sh type.first found in nowhere}} nowhere.

**The order of the steps is not observable.** Simple class names are unique
among the packages any source can see: of {{tr type names}} type names in all
{{ps sources}} sources, {{tr with candidates in more than one package}} have a
candidate in more than one visible package, and {{tr unknown}} resolve nowhere
(that includes the {{tr type names in source_only}} type names of the sources
without a class). Only two simple names exist twice in the corpus at all
(`SL_Tuners_6_channel_NOS_rail` and `SL_Tuners_methanol_fuel_rail`, in two
engine packages each, never both visible).

The runtime library (class files without source under `system/scripts`)
provides: `java.lang` ({{pk java.lang}} classes: `Object`, `String`,
`Integer`, `Float`, `Number`, `Math`, `System`, `Thread`, `Class`, `Vector3`,
`Ypr`, `Pori`, `GameType`, `Native`, `Agent`, `Signal`, `Debug`,
`GarbageCollector` and the interfaces `Runnable`, `Callback`), `java.io`
({{pk java.io}}), `java.util` ({{pk java.util}}), `java.util.resource`
({{pk java.util.resource}}), `java.render` ({{pk java.render}}),
`java.render.osd` ({{pk java.render.osd}}), `java.render.osd.dialog`
({{pk java.render.osd.dialog}}), `java.sound` ({{pk java.sound}}), `java.net`
({{pk java.net}}).

### 7.3 Class names in expressions

In `GameLogic.gameMode`, `Float.toString(x)`, `Dialog.DF_MODAL` the first
identifier is a class. The compiler emits a `class` carrier as the first path
segment. For all {{sh head.name paths starting with a class node}} such paths of
the consistent pairs the class is the first identifier resolved by the rule of
7.2 ({{sh head.class prefix that is another class than the resolver finds}}
exceptions). No identifier that resolves to a class is compiled as a local or
a field instead ({{sh head.first identifier is also a class name but compiled as a variable}}
cases), so the corpus does not show which wins when a variable has the name
of a visible class. How the compiler tells a local from a field from a class,
and when a call path ends in a method ref or in a bare name, is symbol
resolution and belongs to the code generator (`foundation.md` question 4).

### 7.4 Where the named classes live

The exe loads classes through nine classpath directories (`docs/ENGINE.md`
4.2): `java.lang`, `java.net`, `java.io`, `java.util`, `java.render`,
`java.sound` under `system\scripts\`, `java.game` in `sl\scripts\game`,
`java.game.parts` in `parts\scripts`, `java.game.parts.accessories` in
`parts\accessories\scripts`, sub-packages in sub-directories.
`study_class_dirs.py` checks how far that reaches. Per source and distinct
class name used ({{d references}} in all):

| The class is | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| declared in the same file | {{d same file}} |
| in the classpath directory of its package | {{d classpath directory of its package}} |
| in the directory of the source, which is not on the classpath | {{d directory of the source (not on the classpath)}} |
| in another directory that is not on the classpath | {{d another directory (not on the classpath)}} |

The last row is engine parts that name the model classes of cars
(`parts/engines/Baiern_Emer/scripts/src/Baiern_88er_crankshaft.java` uses
`Baiern_models.MODEL_COUPESPORT_GT_III`, class `java.game.cars.Baiern_models`
in `cars/racers/Baiern_data/scripts`).
{{dc pair_940.another directory (not on the classpath)}} of these uses are in
sources with a Build 940 class file, so the exe's compiler found those
classes; **how is unknown** (a
class already loaded by the game when the part was compiled is the likely
answer). A compiler linked into the engine needs an index of every `scripts`
directory, not only the classpath: {{dx off_classpath}} of the {{dx packages}}
packages of the corpus have classes outside the classpath directory of their
name, and {{dx targets}} distinct classes are named from such a foreign
directory.

## 8. What a Java compiler would reject

Syntax first (a Java *parser* stops), then what parses in Java but does not
compile, where it matters to someone writing this compiler. Counts are over
all {{ps sources}} sources; three examples each.

### 8.1 Resource id literals: {{c note.rid-literal}} in {{f note.rid-literal}} sources

{{ex note.rid-literal}}

### 8.2 A `;` in place of the body of a method that is neither `native` nor `abstract`: {{c note.body-semicolon}}

{{c constructor.body ";"}} constructors and {{c method.body ";"}} methods end in
`;` ({{c method.native}} of the methods are native, which is Java).

{{ex note.body-semicolon}}

### 8.3 `import` with a colon: {{c note.import-colon}}

{{ex note.import-colon}}

### 8.4 `native` and `abstract` fields: {{c note.native-field}} + {{c note.abstract-field}}

{{ex note.native-field}}

{{ex note.abstract-field}}

The flags are stored as written (0x48 and 0x10). `installSet` is backed by the
engine (`docs/ENGINE.md` 4.3).

### 8.5 Several public classes in one file: {{c unit.several public types}} sources

{{c unit.public type not named like the file}} public classes are not named like
their file.

{{ex unit.several public types}}

### 8.6 Rejected by Java's checks, accepted here

| What | Count | Java says |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| condition that is a name, a field, an array element or a call | {{c cond.all: name or field}} + {{c cond.all: array element}} + {{c cond.all: call}} | not boolean (unless the type is) |
| condition that is an assignment | {{c odd.assignment as a condition}} | not boolean |
| condition that is an int literal, `x--` or an arithmetic / bitwise expression | {{c cond.all: int literal}} + {{c cond.all: postfix}} + {{c cond.all: arithmetic or bitwise operator}} | not boolean |
| float literal without `f` | {{c form.float d.d}} | a `double`; cannot be assigned to `float` |
| statement after `return` / `break` / `continue` in the same block | {{c odd.statement after return / break / continue in one block}} | unreachable statement |
| local declared again in a later group of the same `switch` | {{c odd.local declared in two groups of one switch}} | already defined |
| local that reuses the name of a live outer local or parameter | {{c odd.local hides an outer local or parameter}} | already defined |
| `final` field without initialiser | {{c odd.final field without initialiser}} | not initialised |
| method declared twice with the same parameter types | {{c odd.method declared twice with the same parameter types}} | already defined |
| import of a package or class that does not exist | {{c import.of a package without classes}} | package does not exist |

{{ex cond.if: name or field}}

{{ex odd.assignment as a condition}}

{{ex cond.while: int literal}}

{{ex odd.statement after return / break / continue in one block}}

{{ex odd.local declared in two groups of one switch}}

{{ex odd.local hides an outer local or parameter}}

{{ex odd.final field without initialiser}}

{{ex odd.method declared twice with the same parameter types}}

{{ex import.of a package without classes}}

For the compiler this means: no type check stands between the parser and the
code generator for conditions; unreachable statements are compiled like any
other (`foundation.md`: nothing is removed); a duplicate method gets a second
row (rule M1 counts both); a local that hides another gets a new slot.

Things Java would also reject and that do **not** occur: a value returned from
a `void` method or a constructor ({{z odd.return with a value in a void method}},
{{z odd.return with a value in a constructor}}), `return;` in a method with a
result type ({{z odd.return without a value in a method with a result type}}), a
method with a result type whose body does not end in `return`
({{z odd.method with a result type whose body does not end with return}}), a field
or parameter name used twice ({{z odd.field declared twice in one class}},
{{z odd.two parameters with one name}}), a local declared twice in one scope
({{z odd.local declared twice in one scope}}).

### 8.7 Legal Java that is easy to overlook

Constructs a Java parser accepts and that are rare enough to be forgotten in a
hand-written compiler; each occurs in a source that has a class file.

| Construct | Count |
| --- | ---: |
| chained assignment `a = b = c` | {{c assign.as a value in assigned value}} |
| assignment as an argument, `f(x = g())` | {{c assign.as a value in argument}} |
| assignment in parentheses as the receiver of `.name` | {{c fieldaccess.on parenthesised assign}} |
| `!--x` | {{c unary.! applied to unary --}} |
| unary `+` in front of a literal; in front of another expression (`a + + b`) | {{c unary.+ applied to float literal}}; {{c unary.+ applied to an expression}} |
| field initialiser with a side effect, `static final int A = ++n;` | {{c field.initialiser is unary ++}} |
| cast of `null` | {{c cast.followed by 'null'}} |
| call on a new object, `new T(a).f();` | {{c call.on new object}} |
| index of a call result, `f(x)[0]` | {{c index.of call result}} |
| `for(;;)` | {{c for.without condition}} |
| case value in parentheses, `case(X):`; a sum as case value | {{c switch.case value written in parentheses}}; {{c switch.case value is Binary}} |
| hex literal with `0X` | {{c form.int hex 0X}} |
| a block as a statement of its own | {{c stmt.Block as a statement of its own}} |
| method or field without access modifier | {{c method.modifiers []}}; {{c field.modifiers []}} |

{{ex fieldaccess.on parenthesised assign}}

{{ex unary.! applied to unary --}}

{{ex unary.+ applied to an expression}}

{{ex cast.followed by 'null'}}

{{ex call.on new object}}

{{ex index.of call result}}

