## 10. Proofs

Reproduce with `python -I lib/run_language.py` (about 3 minutes; `--fast`
skips the sensitivity run). Outputs are in `notes/`: `parse_check.txt`,
`members_check.txt`, `shape_check.txt`, `sensitivity.txt`,
`grammar_census.txt`, `class_dirs.txt`, each with a `.json` twin.

### 10.1 Every source tokenizes and parses (`check_parse.py`)

{{table rules_parse}}

P3 is the strong one: `unparse.py` regenerates the token sequence from the AST
alone, and it equals the lexer's for all {{ps tokens}} tokens. The AST
therefore keeps everything of the source except comments and white space
(parentheses are `Paren` nodes), and no two token sequences share an AST.

Failures: none. (`check_parse.py` prints file, line, column and the construct
for each; the list is empty.)

N1 is a measurement, not a rule: it shows that the parser is strict and not
merely tolerant. Blanking out a single `;`, brace, parenthesis or bracket
makes the source unparsable in {{p N1}} sampled cases. No source has a
redundant one of these tokens (there is no empty statement and no stray `;`,
section 3.4), so an accepted deletion must leave another valid program, and
the one that exists does: in `shock_absorber_Baiern_CS_front.java` line 50,
`rebound_factor = ((Slider)m.gadget).value` followed without `;` by
`((Slider)m.gadget).changeVLabelText(...)` reads as a call of `.value(...)`.

